When stepping through the full codec pipeline, several design decisions become apparent.\ref{fig:codecbd} 
The two priorities were minimizing bit rate and compute. We deliberately implemented GLX as an all-integer-based codec. This avoids costly Floating Point Operations (FLOP), which requires a dedicated Floating Point Unit (FPU) for efficient calculations. 

\subsection{Sampling}
We implemented a 31-tap windowed sinc filter before decimation. As speech is concentrated below 8 kHz, a LPF gives a flat passband below 8 kHz and a stopband above to filter out unwanted frequencies.  Assuming or upsampling to a 48khz input, we simply take every third sample to achieve a lower sample rate with no FLOP necessary.

On the bitrate front, we focused on implementing 3-bit audio. While 3-bit quantization typically introduces harsh, signal-dependent distortion, we alleviate this through subtractive dithering. 
3 bits was chosen in part due to the compute restrictions, but additionally to decrease bitrate and minimize transmission costs. At lower bitrates, parametric coding begins to overtake waveform coding in efficacy and removes the whole point of this codec, while higher bitrates …. 

\subsection{$\mu$-law}
μ-Law companding remains one of the most effective methods for redistributing the Signal-to-Quantization-Noise Ratio (SQNR) in speech applications\cite{smith57}. 

We adopt the $\mu = 255$ law of ITU-T G.711~\cite{itut1988}, 
\begin{equation} 
F(x) = \operatorname{sgn}(x)\,
 \frac{\ln\!\left(1 + \mu|x|\right)}{\ln\!\left(1 + \mu\right)}, 
\qquad |x| \le 1, 
\label{eq:mulaw} 
\end{equation} 

\subsection{Dither}
We implement dither using a 32-bit Linear Feedback Shift Register (LFSR), which enables reconstruction of the pseudo-random noise at the decoder via a shared state seed transmitted within the.GLX header. While dither theory assumes noise to be perfectly random, a LFSR-based approach is not only already widely accepted due to difficulties achieving true randomness \cite{Pamarti07}, but subtractive dither requires. This approach is highly efficient, requiring only one bit shift and three XOR operations per value. Utilizing a primitive tap provides $2^{32}-1$ distinct states. We draw from the LFSR twice per sample, once for the gate and once for the amplitude. This allows for 37 hours of unique dither without repetition.

To prevent clipping resulting from dither-induced amplitude fluctuations, we introduce a headroom factor:

$\frac{1}{1 + \Delta}$

where $\Delta = \text{step}/2^{15}$ is the quantizer step normalized to full scale. Each companded sample is prescaled by $h$ before the dither is added, shrinking the signal such that the sum of signal and dither remains within the int16 range, preventing overflowing or clipping. 

Crucially, both the headroom factor and μ-Law expansion are not implemented at the decoder. The requirement at this stage is not perceptual transparency but error statistics. The headroom is a constant gain, and thus we can save compute. However, because μ-Law is a non-linear process, any expansion or reversion would destroy the statistical properties of the dithered signal. Maintaining the non-linear state ensures we do not violate the assumption of a uniform quantization error $\epsilon$.

\subsection*{Coding}

Successive speech samples remain highly correlated with (first-order intersample correlation ≈ 0.9 [X]), so encoding the residual provides a cheap transform to reduce the signal's dynamic range and increase compressibility \cite{hasegawa03}.
We selected Huffman coding because it offers a favorable balance between compression efficiency, implementation complexity, and performance for this application. For 3-bit audio deltas, the information conveyed within a static Huffman codebook can be transmitted in just 2 bytes,\footnote{We precompute a full table, then transmit the bits and alpha value.} minimizing the storage overhead.
Using a precomputed codebook enables both encoding and decoding in linear time with minimal computational overhead. Compared to adaptive entropy coders, Huffman coding avoids transmitting or reconstructing codebooks at runtime, while also requiring significantly less memory than table-based coders with larger lookup structures. Although entropy coders such as tANS can achieve compression closer to the theoretical optimum, they introduce additional implementation complexity and computational overhead.
To better match different source distributions, we precomputed three static Huffman codebooks using modeled probability mass functions parameterized by $\alpha \in {0, 0.5, 1}$. This allows the encoder to select a codebook that better reflects the expected distribution of audio deltas under different dithering settings.
\subsection{.GLX}

We implemented this into a working codec designated under the format specifier \verb|.glx|. While retaining many of the features of a .Wav header

In exploring parametric dither, we allow for a trade-off between whitening and (de-correlation) and compressibility. Standard dithering whitens the noise, but reduces the effectiveness of compression. By parameterizing the shape and amplitude of the dither, the encoder can be tuned between sufficient signal decorrelation to maintain ASR performance and lowering the entropy of the residual stream to maximize the compressibility of final transmission \cite{murray2026}. At the barest level, our codec was designed to produce identical output both in software and in an embedded RISC-V build without significant compute.


